<description>
Recover each partially hidden 6x6 ring sprite and stack complete copies from top layer to bottom layer.

Input structure:
- The dominant color is the background.
- Several non-background colors are visible fragments of the same 12-pixel
  outline ring in a 6x6 box; small off-template marker pixels are noise.
- Ring fragments overlap, so upper rings replace some expected pixels of
  lower rings, and a ring may also be clipped by the input boundary.

Rule:
- Identify every substantial color whose pixels all lie on one translated
  copy of the ring template.
- For each missing in-bounds template pixel, the color currently occupying
  that pixel is above the hidden ring.
- Topologically sort these occlusion relations from top to bottom, then draw
  one complete 6x6 ring for each color in that order on a background canvas.

Key insight:
- The output is not a crop of the scene; it is the layer stack of complete
  ring sprites reconstructed from their visible fragments.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_in_order",
  "src": "visible fragments of overlapping 6x6 outline-ring sprites; sparse off-template colors are marker noise",
  "dst": "vertical stack of one completed ring sprite per recovered color",
  "transform": "complete every fragment to the 12-pixel ring template using the input background",
  "multi_step": "order sprites from top layer to bottom layer: if a color occupies another sprite's expected ring pixel, it is placed earlier",
  "grid_shape": "6 columns; height is 6 times the number of recovered ring sprites"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10-20 rows x 10-20 cols, always square [observed]
  background: single dominant color filling most of the grid
  ring_template: |
    fixed 12-pixel outline in a 6x6 box:
      ..XX..
      .X..X.
      X....X
      X....X
      .X..X.
      ..XX..
  rings:
    count: 2-4, each a distinct non-bg color
    placement: each ring translated to a unique offset within the grid
    overlap: every adjacent pair in the layer order must share >=1 ring-template pixel;
             overlap graph must be connected; occlusion edges must yield a unique total order
    layer_order: if two rings overlap on a template pixel, the visible/covering color is earlier
                 than the hidden/covered color; this front-to-back order determines the output
                 stack and is not a sort by the rings' vertical grid positions
    visibility: each ring has 8-12 visible pixels (hidden by higher rings or grid clipping)
    clipping: 0-1 rings may extend past grid boundary (0-2 pixels clipped) [observed]
    topmost_ring: fully visible (12 pixels) unless clipped by grid boundary
  noise:
    colors: 1-2 distinct colors, each different from bg and all ring colors
    pixels_per_color: 2-5, scattered individually on bg cells (not on any ring pixel)
  output: 6-wide, height = 6 * ring_count; each 6x6 block is one complete ring in layer color,
          stacked from top/front layer to bottom/back layer according to coverage precedence,
          filled with bg elsewhere

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick ring_count (2-4), bg color, and ring_count distinct non-bg ring colors.
    2. Choose a square grid size N (10-20) large enough to fit all rings with overlap.
    3. Build the layered scene front-to-back; place the output's first/top block ring first at a random valid offset.
    4. For each subsequent ring, pick an offset that overlaps >=1 pixel with at least one
       already-placed ring and keeps >=8 ring pixels uncovered by all higher rings
       (accounting for grid clipping). Verify unique coverage-derived topological sort after each placement.
    5. Render input by painting rings back-to-front (bottom layer first) onto a bg grid,
       so higher layers naturally overwrite lower ones at shared positions.
    6. Scatter 1-2 noise colors (2-5 pixels each) on remaining bg cells.
    7. Build the output by stacking complete 6x6 ring sprites top/front-to-bottom/back on a bg canvas.
```
</generation_spec>
